/**
 * @page topic_dependencies Dependencies
 * @ingroup module_development
 * @brief Libraries and tools used by the current build.
 *
 * CMake can use installed packages or the repository's source dependency cache.
 * The build selects only the features enabled for the target platform; a docs
 * build does not configure the native dependency graph.
 *
 * @section dependencies_runtime Runtime libraries
 *
 * The main application uses FFmpeg for media input/decoding/encoding, PortAudio
 * for native audio, libwebsockets for WebSocket transport, ngtcp2/nghttp3 and
 * TLS support for QUIC/WebTransport where enabled, SQLite for discovery state,
 * and platform libraries for terminal, threading, and device access.
 *
 * Other project dependencies include libsodium/BearSSL or the selected crypto
 * backend, zstd compression, utf8proc text handling, and the SIMD/rendering
 * support selected by the platform build.
 *
 * @section dependencies_build Build sources
 *
 * `ASCIICHAT_BUILD_DEPS_FROM_SOURCE` controls source-built dependency selection
 * when packages are unavailable or a reproducible cache is desired. Submodules
 * must be initialized for native builds that use vendored dependencies. The
 * docs-only CMake project needs only Doxygen and the documentation theme.
 *
 * @section dependencies_platform Platform differences
 *
 * Windows uses the repository's Clang/Ninja path and native console/audio
 * backends. Linux and macOS use their platform terminal/device layers. iOS
 * builds use the reduced static library path and omit desktop-only subsystems;
 * see @ref topic_build for the current Apple workflow.
 *
 * @section dependencies_troubleshooting Troubleshooting
 *
 * Inspect the CMake configure summary before changing flags. Clear only the
 * affected dependency cache after a failed source build, and never mix a stale
 * submodule directory with a different checkout. For documentation, configure
 * `cmake -S docs` instead of running the full native configure.
 *
 * @see topic_build
 * @see topic_testing
 */
